 third in 1993 and runners-up in 1995. Swiss brothers " [:a {:href "/athletes/10700"} "Peter"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10701"} "Ueli Matti"] " were the reigning European champions, while Poland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10490"} "Krzysztof Kołomański"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10525"} "Michał Staniszewski"] " were the most recent World Champions."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Kołomański and Staniszewski performed the best of these pairs in the first run, finishing in fourth place, while the Matti brothers were eighth, Adisson and Forgues were tenth, and Šimek and Rohan were 14th. The top three spots went to " [:a {:href "/athletes/9903"} "André Ehrenberg"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9983"} "Michael Senft"] " of Germany, twin brothers " [:a {:href "/athletes/9408"} "Petr"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9409"} "Pavel Štercl"] " of the Czech Republic, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9621"} "Emmanuel Del Rey"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9674"} "Thierry Saïdi"] " of France. Of all of these teams, only the Štercl brothers failed to post a better mark in the second round, with the most dramatic increase being that of Šimek and Rohan, who went from 14th to a silver medal; only Adisson and Forgues bettered their score. Ehrenberg and Senft, meanwhile, improved enough to stave off Del Rey and Saïdi’s challenge and remain on the podium with bronze."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Bib"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Total Time"] [:th {} "Run #1"] [:th {} "Run #2"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/9589"} "Franck Adisson"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/9629"} "Wilfrid Forgues"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] "FRA"]] [:td {} "158.82"] [:td {} "192.85 (10)"] [:td {} "158.82 (1)"] [: